    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_rdp_111'), 'The shipped write path and the size of its key:

const raw = await redis.get("leaderboard:global");
const board: Record<string, number> = raw ? JSON.parse(raw) : {};
board[userId] = (board[userId] ?? 0) + points;
await redis.set("leaderboard:global", JSON.stringify(board));

$ redis-cli STRLEN leaderboard:global
(integer) 2097152

A reviewer proposes a hash with one field per learner instead: HINCRBY leaderboard:global <userId> <points>.

Statement: "With the hash, GET /leaderboard?top=10 can read just the ten best learners from Redis instead of all 40,000 entries."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. The hash fixes the write path: HINCRBY changes one field on the server, sends a few bytes and cannot lose a concurrent update. But a hash has no order. Redis cannot return "the ten largest fields" of a hash, so the top-10 endpoint would still need HGETALL (all 40,000 fields) and a sort in Node.js on every request. The structure whose cheap operations include both "add points to one member" and "read the top N" is a sorted set: ZINCRBY and ZRANGE ... REV (or ZREVRANGE), O(log N) plus the ten members returned. The misconception is that splitting a big string into fields solves every access pattern; it solves the ones that touch a single field.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_rdp_111') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
