1792004400.101 [0 10.0.1.5:51234] "GET" "leaderboard:global"
1792004400.103 [0 10.0.1.6:40022] "GET" "leaderboard:global"
1792004400.140 [0 10.0.1.5:51234] "SET" "leaderboard:global" "{...\"u_88\":430,\"u_91\":120...}"
1792004400.142 [0 10.0.1.6:40022] "SET" "leaderboard:global" "{...\"u_88\":400,\"u_91\":150...}"

Both SET commands replied OK.

Statement: "After these four commands, the +30 points that instance 10.0.1.5 added for u_88 are no longer in Redis."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. Both instances read the same version of the string (u_88 = 400). Instance .5 wrote 430 for u_88; instance .6, which had read the old version, then wrote its own copy with u_88 = 400 and u_91 = 150, replacing the whole value. Both writes succeeded, and one update is gone. Redis executes each command atomically, but a GET followed by a SET from one client is not atomic: other clients'' commands run in between. ZINCRBY (or HINCRBY) performs the read-modify-write inside one command, and MULTI/EXEC with WATCH or a Lua script can make several commands act as one. The misconception is that because Redis is single-threaded, application code that reads and then writes is safe.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_rdp_111') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
