    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_rdp_121'), 'The deploy at 10:00:00 warmed the 500 most-viewed courses with SET course:<id> <json> EX 300. At 10:01:53:

$ for id in c_12 c_13 c_14 c_15; do redis-cli TTL course:$id; done
(integer) 187
(integer) 187
(integer) 187
(integer) 187

pg_stat_statements, calls per minute of the course lookup:
10:04  410
10:05  23,900
10:06  380
10:09  395
10:10  24,100

Statement: "Storing each course with EX 300 plus a random 0 to 60 seconds would turn these spikes into misses spread over about a minute."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. All 500 keys were written within the same second with the same TTL, so they expire within the same second (TTL 187 on every key) and the misses reach PostgreSQL together, every 300 seconds. With a random 0-60 s added per key, keys written together expire at different moments across a 61-second window; once reloaded they get new random TTLs, so the waves do not line up again. Lowering the TTL would only make the spikes more frequent. Note what jitter does not do: it spreads many keys apart, but it does not protect a single very hot key from many concurrent misses (a stampede, lesson 11). The misconception is that the TTL value is the problem; the problem is that the expiry times are identical.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_rdp_121') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
