WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_rdp_121'), 'getCourse caches "not found" as the JSON null for 30 seconds and courses for 300 + jitter. Nothing invalidates the cache when an admin publishes a course. redis-cli MONITOR and the admin log:

10:00:05.120 "GET" "course:c_207"
10:00:05.124 "SET" "course:c_207" "null" "EX" "30"      (learner A: 404)
10:00:20.000 admin publishes c_207 (INSERT committed in PostgreSQL)
10:00:31.870 "GET" "course:c_207"                        (learner B: 404)

Statement: "Learner C, who opens /courses/c_207 at 10:00:40, also gets a 404, because the not-found entry lasts 30 seconds from the moment the course was published."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. A TTL counts from the command that set it, here the SET at 10:00:05.124, so the not-found entry expires at 10:00:35.124. Redis knows nothing about the INSERT in PostgreSQL at 10:00:20. Learner B at 10:00:31 was still inside the 30 seconds and got the cached 404; learner C at 10:00:40 misses the cache, getCourse loads c_207 from PostgreSQL and returns it (and caches it with the normal TTL). The worst case for a newly published course is therefore the negative TTL after the last not-found answer, which is why the negative TTL is kept short. The misconception is that cache expiry is related to when the data changed in the database; without invalidation the two are independent.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_rdp_121') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
