WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_rdp_231'), 'The AI-written award path, deployed on 12 October:

async awardPoints(e: ScoreEvent) {
  await this.redis.zincrby("leaderboard:global", e.points, e.userId);
}

After the maintenance restart on 26 October:

$ redis-cli CONFIG GET save
1) "save"
2) ""
$ redis-cli CONFIG GET appendonly
1) "appendonly"
2) "no"
$ redis-cli ZCARD leaderboard:global
(integer) 0

codedev=# SELECT max(created_at) FROM score_events;
          max
------------------------
 2026-10-12 08:59:41+07

Statement: "The points awarded between 12 and 26 October cannot be recovered from Redis or from PostgreSQL."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. save "" disables RDB snapshots and appendonly no disables the AOF, so this Redis keeps data only in memory and a restart starts empty. The new awardPoints never writes score_events, which is why the newest row in PostgreSQL is from just before the deploy. The two weeks of points existed only in Redis memory, and that memory is gone; at best they can be re-derived from another log, such as the graded submissions, if one exists. The misconception is that Redis "has" the data because the leaderboard looked right for two weeks: a cache configured like this is a copy that can disappear at any time, so something durable must hold every event.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_rdp_231') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO challenge.question_options (question_id, option_text, is_correct)
SELECT q.id, o.option_text, o.is_correct
FROM q, (VALUES
        ('True', true),
        ('False', false)
    ) AS o(option_text, is_correct);
WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_rdp_231'), 'To prevent the next loss, a teammate proposes keeping awardPoints exactly as it is and changing only the Redis configuration:

appendonly yes
appendfsync everysec

The instance keeps maxmemory-policy allkeys-lru, as it is also the cache for course pages, and a replica takes over automatically when the primary fails.

Statement: "With this configuration Redis can stay the only place where scores are written: a crash, a failover or memory pressure can no longer lose an awarded point."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. AOF with appendfsync everysec fsyncs about once per second, so a crash can lose roughly the last second of writes, which the Redis documentation states. Replication is asynchronous, so a failover can promote a replica that has not received the last writes. And allkeys-lru may evict leaderboard:global itself when the course cache fills memory. The award path also has no event id, so a retried event is still counted twice. Persistence makes Redis restart faster and lose less; it does not turn a cache into the system of record. Write each score event to PostgreSQL first, idempotently by event id, and treat the sorted set as a copy you can rebuild. The misconception is that "persistent" means "durable and correct".', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_rdp_231') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
