WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_rdp_311'), 'redis-cli on staging. u_1001 reached 90 points on Monday at 09:12, u_2040 reached 90 points two hours later, and the API called ZADD in that order:

> ZADD lb:week:2026-W44 90 u_1001
(integer) 1
> ZADD lb:week:2026-W44 90 u_2040
(integer) 1
> ZADD lb:week:2026-W44 120 u_0815
(integer) 1
> ZREVRANGE lb:week:2026-W44 0 -1 WITHSCORES
1) "u_0815"
2) "120"
3) "u_2040"
4) "90"
5) "u_1001"
6) "90"

Statement: "u_2040 is listed above u_1001 because, when scores are equal, Redis puts the member that was added most recently first."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. A sorted set stores only members and scores, not when a member was added. Members with equal scores are ordered by their bytes (lexicographically): ZRANGE lists u_1001 before u_2040, and ZREVRANGE reverses the whole order, so u_2040 comes first. Calling ZADD in the opposite order gives exactly the same output. If the product wants "first to reach the score ranks higher", that rule has to be encoded in the score itself (or the tie broken in PostgreSQL); insertion order will not do it. The misconception is that a sorted set behaves like a list that remembers arrival order.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_rdp_311') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
