WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_rdp_321'), 'The consumer applies { learnerId, totalScore, version } with this script (KEYS[1] = lb:all, KEYS[2] = lb:ver, ARGV = learnerId, totalScore, version):

local stored = redis.call(''HGET'', KEYS[2], ARGV[1])
if stored and stored >= ARGV[3] then
  return ''skipped''
end
redis.call(''ZADD'', KEYS[1], ARGV[2], ARGV[1])
redis.call(''HSET'', KEYS[2], ARGV[1], ARGV[3])
return ''applied''

Consumer log for u_2290:

10:41:07.112 v9  total 300 -> applied
10:41:52.840 v10 total 340 -> skipped
10:42:30.005 v11 total 380 -> skipped

> HGET lb:ver u_2290
"9"

Statement: "Versions 10 and 11 are skipped because the script compares the versions as strings, and \"9\" sorts after \"10\" and \"11\"."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. HGET returns a bulk string and ARGV values are strings, so Lua''s >= compares them character by character: "9" >= "10" is true because ''9'' comes after ''1''. Every version from 10 to 89 now looks older than 9, so the learner''s score is frozen at 300 until version 90. The fix is tonumber() on both sides (or storing a fixed-width version). The misconception is that Redis knows the hash value is a number: everything in a hash is a string, and the comparison follows the types of the language that does it.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_rdp_321') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
