WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_rdp_321'), 'A reviewer suggests dropping the script and doing the check in TypeScript, because "every Redis command is atomic anyway":

const stored = Number(await redis.hget("lb:ver", u.learnerId) ?? 0);
if (u.version > stored) {
  await redis.zadd("lb:all", u.totalScore, u.learnerId);
  await redis.hset("lb:ver", u.learnerId, String(u.version));
}

Timeline with two consumer workers (u_2290, stored version 14, total 340):

t1 worker A  HGET lb:ver u_2290 -> "14"   (A holds v16, total 410)
t2 worker B  HGET lb:ver u_2290 -> "14"   (B holds v15, total 380)
t3 worker A  ZADD lb:all 410 u_2290; HSET lb:ver u_2290 16
t4 worker B  ZADD lb:all 380 u_2290; HSET lb:ver u_2290 15

Statement: "Because each of HGET, ZADD and HSET is atomic, this version of the code cannot end with an older total on the board."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. Each command is atomic on its own, but the check (HGET) and the write (ZADD, HSET) are separate round trips, and another worker can run in between. At t2 worker B read version 14 before A wrote 16, so B still believes v15 is newer and overwrites 410 with 380 - and even moves the stored version back to 15. Check and write must be one unit: a Lua script (EVAL/EVALSHA), or WATCH lb:ver + MULTI/EXEC with a retry when EXEC returns null. The misconception is that atomic commands make a sequence of commands atomic.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_rdp_321') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
