Statement: "u_2041 went from 1310 back to 1240 because the consumer writes each event''s total with ZADD in the order the messages arrive, so the redelivered version 17 overwrote version 18: the last write wins."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. The consumer in D never compares anything: whatever arrives is written. ZADD on an existing member replaces its score, so the sorted set always holds the total of the last delivery, not of the newest event. Version 18 (1310) was written at 10:00:03.118 and the late copy of version 17 (1240) at 10:00:05.405, so 1240 stayed. The hypothesis explains every piece of evidence: the redelivery in A, the order of writes in C, the drop in rank in B and the correct row in PostgreSQL, which never saw the redelivery. The misconception is to think a write of a total is safe to repeat in any order; it is safe to repeat, not safe to reorder.', 2, 5
