Statement: "The clocks of api-1 and api-2 are skewed, so Redis sorted the two ZADD commands for u_3307 by the wrong timestamp; syncing the hosts with NTP would stop the score from going backwards."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. Redis does not order commands by any client timestamp: it executes them in the order they arrive, and MONITOR, stamped by Redis''s own clock, shows 1025 written before 990. The consumer in D does not read published or any other time either, so no clock can be to blame. The order that matters is the version from learner_score: 9 is older than 10, and that counter comes from one row in one database, independent of any host clock. evt_e31 was simply processed after evt_e40 because two consumers work in parallel. That is also why the fix compares versions rather than timestamps: ordering by wall-clock time across hosts is exactly what clock skew can break.', 4, 5
